<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>WinExe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <BuiltInComInteropSupport>true</BuiltInComInteropSupport>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Deliberately outside the build: this project is not in OpenTabletArtist.slnx, no workflow builds it,
    and Dependabot does not cover it. That is a decision (#779), not neglect.

    The prerequisite checks it used to carry - udev rules, conflicting kernel modules, HID permissions -
    now live in the app's health system (#780), where a user whose tablet is not detected will actually
    see them. What is left here is the privileged fix implementation: pkexec writing udev rules,
    blacklisting modules, regenerating the initramfs, and usermod -aG input. OTA deliberately does not do
    any of that - the health copy names the commands instead - so this is kept as reference for whoever
    revisits that call, not as shipping code.

    So the versions below are pinned on purpose. Nothing builds this, so there is nothing for a bump to be
    current for, and modernising it would imply a support commitment that does not exist. If you are here
    from a dependency triage: leave it. If you are here to revive the fixers, note that FixUdevRules
    cannot work outside a repo checkout as written - it locates generate-rules.sh by walking up to .git,
    which the release does not ship.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Avalonia" Version="11.3.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Avalonia.Desktop" Version="11.3.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Avalonia.Themes.Fluent" Version="11.3.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Avalonia.Fonts.Inter" Version="11.3.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
